---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { FORMAT_REFERENCE, PRIMARY_FORMATS, type FormatReference } from '../content/format-reference';
const formats = PRIMARY_FORMATS
  .map((id) => FORMAT_REFERENCE[id])
  .filter((format): format is FormatReference => Boolean(format));
---
<SiteLayout title="Supported Image Formats | ConvertAnyFile" description="Explore the certified image output formats in ConvertAnyFile, from JPG, PNG, WebP and AVIF to JXL, JP2, TIFF, ICO, TGA, HDR, EXR and more.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span>Formats</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Format reference</span><h1>Choose the right image format for the job.</h1><p>Image conversion is the first production category on ConvertAnyFile. The converter scans the packaged ImageMagick runtime, then exposes only the output formats that are both writable and included in our browser-tested product catalog.</p></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Start with the destination</span><h2>Format choice is about the job, not the extension.</h2><p>A photograph, screenshot, transparent logo, phone original, scanned document, texture, HDR frame, and favicon have different requirements. Choosing the right output means balancing compatibility, file size, transparency, editing needs, and what the next application actually accepts.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>How support works</strong><p>ConvertAnyFile does not trust a filename extension alone. The image workflow checks file signatures, verifies runtime decoder support, scans runtime writers, and only publishes outputs that are on the certified conversion matrix.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Certified outputs</span><h2>{formats.length} image output formats in the production catalog.</h2><p>Every format below is still filtered against the WASM runtime running in the browser. If a deployed build cannot write a codec, the converter hides that option rather than offering a conversion that cannot complete.</p></div><div class="format-grid">{formats.map((format)=><article class="format-card" id={format.id}><span>{format.name}</span><h3>{format.role}</h3><p>{format.summary}</p><p><strong>Best for:</strong> {format.bestFor}</p><p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> {format.tradeoffs}</p></article>)}</div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Quick comparison</span><h2>Compression, transparency, and compatibility.</h2><p>No single column decides the winner. The right output depends on the content and destination.</p></div><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Compression</th><th>Transparency</th><th>Compatibility note</th></tr></thead><tbody>{formats.map((format)=><tr><td><strong>{format.name}</strong></td><td>{format.compression}</td><td>{format.transparency}</td><td>{format.compatibility}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">Practical rules</span><h2>Use mainstream outputs unless the destination asks for something else.</h2><ul class="content-checklist"><li>Use JPG when a photograph needs broad compatibility and a compact delivery size.</li><li>Use PNG when transparency, screenshots, sharp text, or lossless graphics matter.</li><li>Use WebP or AVIF when modern web delivery is the goal and your real pipeline supports them well.</li><li>Use JXL, JP2, PCX, TGA, QOI, PNM, HDR, or EXR when the destination workflow explicitly requires that specialist format.</li></ul></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>HEIC is input-only today</strong><p>Compatible HEIC/HEIF photos can be decoded and converted to supported outputs, but the current packaged WASM build does not report a HEIC writer, so HEIC is intentionally not listed as a certified output.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">Single-image conversion policy</span><h2>One downloadable image per input file.</h2><p>Animated GIF/WebP, multi-page TIFF, layered sources, and multi-size icon containers can contain more than a single flattened image. The current converter deliberately produces one image per input rather than silently promising preservation of timelines, pages, layers, or every icon size.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>Input compatibility</strong><p>The image engine recognizes additional camera and specialist image extensions beyond the outputs above. Actual decoding depends on the runtime delegate and the specific file, especially for camera RAW families.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">RAW and less common inputs</span><h2>Recognition is not the same as guaranteed decoding.</h2><p>Two RAW files with related extensions can still require different decoder support because camera models and file variants differ. Input support is therefore capability-derived rather than a promise that every file carrying a known extension will convert successfully. Camera RAW remains a best-effort input path until real-camera fixtures have been certified model by model.</p></div><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/image-converter/">Open Image Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/guides/jpg-vs-png/">JPG vs PNG guide <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/guides/webp-vs-avif/">WebP vs AVIF guide <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/guides/heic-explained/">HEIC input guide <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
